Notes
-----

aws-lc-sys 0.46.0 builds the C code of AWS-LC 5.11.0 into tukuyomi-proxy and
tukuyomi-records-ship. Its LICENSE file (text 6) lists the parts of AWS-LC
with their copyright notices and licenses, and contains the full texts of
those licenses. The parts that are compiled in are listed above with the
license under which each is used. The parts that the file lists as used only
for testing and build tooling are not included.

Jitter Entropy RNG: its own notice (text 105), taken from the comment at the
top of its source file jitterentropy.h with the comment marks removed,
offers its BSD-style license or, alternatively, the GNU General Public
License. It is used under the BSD-3-Clause license, which AWS-LC also elects
in its LICENSE file (text 6).

regex-syntax 0.8.11 is used under MIT (text 8). The Unicode tables in the
crate (src/unicode_tables) are under the Unicode data files license,
Unicode-DFS-2016 (text 64).

keyed_priority_queue 0.4.2: the crate package contains no license file. Its
Cargo.toml gives the license as "MIT" and the author as AngelicosPhosphoros.
The MIT License text 1, which contains no copyright line, is given for it.

PCRE2 (text 124): the library is under BSD-3-Clause with the exemption for
binary library-like packages, and SLJIT, the code of its JIT compiler, is
under BSD-2-Clause.

Rust standard library: it is licensed under "Apache-2.0 OR MIT" and is used
under MIT. The MIT License file of the toolchain has only the placeholder
copyright line of the license template, so the MIT License text 1, which
contains no copyright line, is given for it. The copyright notices of the
standard library in the toolchain say: "Copyrights in the Rust Standard
Library are retained by their contributors." They give the copyright as "The
Rust Project Developers (see https://thanks.rust-lang.org)", and give the
license of library/core/src/unicode/unicode_data.rs as Unicode-3.0 with the
copyright "1991-2024 Unicode, Inc" (text 126).

